\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
If C is an exact complex C*-algebra and one fixed integer $n\geq 1$ makes $h^{\oplus n}$ properly infinite for every $h\in C_+$, must each h itself be properly infinite and C strongly purely infinite? Consequently, in the separable nuclear setting does this fixed-amplification hypothesis imply absorption of $O\infty $?

\subsection{Short English statement}
If C is an exact complex C*-algebra and one fixed integer $n\geq 1$ makes $h^{\oplus n}$ properly infinite for every $h\in C_+$, must each h itself be properly infinite and C strongly purely infinite? Consequently, in the separable nuclear setting does this fixed-amplification hypothesis imply absorption of $O\infty $?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/README.md}.
